{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Class\n\n`src/rebar/_store/lock.py` — **384 `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'flock'` plus\n4 `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'fcntl'`** in job `100874330553`, roughly 74% of the 510\nfailures and the single largest class by an order of magnitude.\n\n`lock.py:72-75` guards the **import** (`try: import fcntl / except ImportError: fcntl = None`) but\nthere is no `is not None` check at either **use** site — `lock.py:207` in `_acquire_fcntl` and\n`lock.py:254` in `write_lock_is_busy`. Every store write reaches `_acquire_once` → `_acquire_fcntl`\n(`lock.py:491`), so on Windows every ticket write raises.\n\nTwo sibling modules DO gate their use sites with `if fcntl is not None:` — `_snapshot/git_fetch.py:79`\nand `_snapshot/janitor.py:430` — which makes `lock.py` an **outlier rather than a deliberate choice**.\n(`reducer/marker.py` is NOT such a sibling: it guards the import but calls `fcntl.flock` unguarded at\n:33/:37, so it shares this defect. That is tracked separately as bug `nitro-heavy-bluefish` and is\nexplicitly OUT of S1's scope — it is the per-ticket archive-marker lock, not the store write lock in\nthis story's file impact.)\n\n## THE OBVIOUS TWO-LINE FIX IS UNSAFE. DO NOT TAKE IT.\n\nSkipping the fcntl leg when `fcntl is None` looks like two lines. It silently breaks a documented,\nload-bearing invariant.\n\n`_acquire_mkdir` (`lock.py:325-362`) passes `fcntl_held=True` into stale-lock reclamation, and its\nown docstring states the precondition verbatim:\n\n> **Precondition (load bearing, hence `fcntl_held=True` below):** this is only ever reached from\n> `acquire` AFTER the exclusive `fcntl.flock(LOCK_EX)` leg has been taken … if that owner were\n> still alive it would still hold this fcntl lock and we could not be here.\n\nDegrade to mkdir-only and `fcntl_held=True` becomes **a lie**. rebar would reclaim a write lock held\nby a **live** process: silent store corruption under concurrency, strictly worse than not supporting\nWindows at all, because the failure is invisible instead of loud.\n\n## What a correct fix requires\n\n1. **A real alternative exclusive leg** on Windows — `msvcrt.locking(fd, LK_NBLCK, n)` (or\n   `LockFileEx` via `ctypes`) — with the same *kernel-mediated, dies-with-the-process* exclusivity\n   `flock` provides. A userspace substitute that does not die with its holder does NOT satisfy the\n   precondition.\n2. **EVERY store write-lock probe routes through that one selector**, not just `acquire`. There are\n   three same-path sites and all three are in scope: `_acquire_fcntl` (`lock.py:207`);\n   `write_lock_is_busy` (`lock.py:254`), an advisory zero-deadline probe that fails OPEN and never\n   touches `fcntl_held`; and `doctor_locks._probe_fcntl` (`_commands/doctor_locks.py:142,148`),\n   the read-only `rebar doctor --locks` probe of the SAME `.ticket-write.lock` — it also calls\n   `fcntl.LOCK_UN`, so the selector exposes a release as well as a take. Fixing only `acquire`\n   leaves the other two raising `AttributeError` on Windows.\n3. **A reworked staleness proof.** The current proof leans on `flock` being kernel-mediated on the\n   inode. The Windows leg's proof must be stated explicitly in the docstring and be its own\n   argument. If the Windows leg cannot support `fcntl_held=True`, reclamation must be narrowed on\n   Windows rather than the flag falsified. Note `lock_owner._pid_alive` uses `os.kill(pid, 0)`,\n   which on Windows is documented to **TerminateProcess** the target rather than probe it — so the\n   liveness probe must be narrowed on Windows too, or reclamation would kill a live holder.\n4. **A test that fails if the invariant is degraded** — the epic's AC5 and `f850`'s AC5, carried\n   forward unchanged.\n5. **The Windows primitive's capability must be VERIFIED, not asserted.** The whole design rests on\n   two properties of `msvcrt.locking`: the lock is visible ACROSS PROCESSES, and the kernel releases\n   it when the holding process dies. A POSIX-host simulation exercises rebar's Windows *branch* but\n   cannot confirm those properties. They are therefore proved by a `windows-latest`-only test\n   (`os.name == \"nt\"`, skipped elsewhere) that runs in the epic's `sweep (windows)` job: a child\n   process takes the leg and the parent must be refused; the child is then killed and the parent\n   must succeed. If that test fails, the design is wrong and the story reopens.\n\n## This story GATES the rest of the epic\n\nUntil store writes work on Windows the other classes cannot be measured — their tests fail during\nstore setup before reaching the semantics under test. The bug's \".EXE console-script invocation\"\nclass is downstream of S1: those `CalledProcessError`s carry this `flock` `AttributeError` as\nstderr. `S1 blocks` every other child of the epic.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] `fcntl` is never dereferenced when it is `None` at any of the three sites in requirement 2;\n      a Windows run reaches the same exclusivity guarantee by a different documented mechanism.\n      Checked by `pytest tests/unit/store/test_windows_exclusive_leg.py -q`, which drives the real\n      `acquire()`, `write_lock_is_busy()` and `doctor_locks._probe_fcntl()` with the `fcntl`\n      primitive stubbed absent and a Windows-shaped primitive supplied.\n- [x] `_acquire_mkdir`'s `fcntl_held=True` is still true under the Windows leg with its own proof\n      written out in the docstring, or is not passed and reclamation is narrowed; and\n      `lock_owner._pid_alive` never calls `os.kill` on a platform where that terminates the target.\n      Checked by `pytest tests/unit/store/test_windows_exclusive_leg.py -k \"precondition or pid\" -q`.\n- [x] A test fails if the exclusivity precondition is degraded: with NO exclusive leg available,\n      `acquire()` raises and never creates `.ticket-write.lock.d`. Checked by\n      `pytest tests/unit/store/test_windows_exclusive_leg.py -k no_leg -q`.\n- [x] The Windows primitive's cross-process exclusivity and release-on-process-death are proved\n      empirically on `windows-latest`, not asserted. Checked by\n      `pytest tests/unit/store/test_windows_exclusive_leg.py -k native -q` on `windows-latest`\n      (self-skipping on POSIX), which is executed by the epic's `sweep (windows)` job.\n- [x] POSIX behaviour is unchanged. Checked by\n      `pytest tests/integration/test_concurrency_regression.py tests/integration/test_store_concurrency.py tests/interfaces/store/test_reads_under_sync_contention.py -q`\n      plus `make lint` and `make typecheck`.\n- [x] No unguarded `fcntl` dereference remains on the store write-lock path, so the sweep's\n      `flock` `AttributeError` count is 0 by construction. Checked IN-SESSION by\n      `grep -nE '(^|[^_[:alnum:]])fcntl\\.' src/rebar/_store/lock.py src/rebar/_store/lock_owner.py src/rebar/_commands/doctor_locks.py`\n      returning NO matches (every surviving `fcntl` call lives in the leg selector behind its\n      `is not None` guard). Confirmed post-merge on the epic's artifact:\n      `gh run view <id> --log --job \"sweep (windows)\" | grep -cE \"NoneType.*flock|named 'fcntl'\"`\n      must print `0`.\n\n## Testing\n\n1. RED first: `pytest tests/unit/store/test_windows_exclusive_leg.py -q` must fail before the fix\n   (the `fcntl`-absent path raises `AttributeError`/`NoneType`).\n2. Platform-seam simulation on the POSIX dev host: the exclusive-leg selector is driven with the\n   `fcntl` primitive stubbed absent and a Windows-shaped primitive supplied, so the Windows branch\n   of the real `acquire()` / `write_lock_is_busy()` runs here rather than being asserted about.\n3. Native capability proof on `windows-latest` (item 5 above) — the only place the real primitive's\n   cross-process and release-on-death properties are established.\n4. Mutation check: deleting the no-leg guard, or negating the leg-selection branch, must turn at\n   least one test RED.\n5. POSIX no-regression: the three concurrency suites named in the Acceptance Criteria, run before\n   and after the change, plus `make lint` and `make typecheck`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788620047242882001,"uuid":"5eabe02b-a755-4006-a618-28899af04b79"}